(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-physiology-master-exam-version-6"]={"title":"Physiology Master Exam — Form B (70 Questions)","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which best describes a functional distinction between plasma proteins and the formed elements of blood?","choices":["Plasma proteins clot the blood on their own, while the formed elements neutralize all invading toxins","Plasma proteins such as albumin generate colloid osmotic pressure, while red cells transport oxygen","Plasma proteins carry oxygen, while the formed elements maintain colloid osmotic pressure","Plasma proteins buffer carbon dioxide, while the formed elements dissolve dietary lipids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Albumin and the other plasma proteins are too large to cross the capillary wall freely, so they hold water in the vasculature and create colloid (oncotic) osmotic pressure; the formed element responsible for oxygen carriage is the red cell, via hemoglobin. The two therefore serve genuinely different roles. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 3-6"},{"q":"The biconcave disc shape of the red blood cell chiefly benefits its function by...","choices":["providing rigidity so the cell resists rupture in high-pressure arteries","maximizing surface area for gas exchange and allowing deformation through narrow capillaries","increasing its internal volume so that it can store a large amount of extra dissolved oxygen","creating a nucleus-rich center that supports rapid ongoing cell division"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The biconcave geometry gives a high surface-area-to-volume ratio (short diffusion distances for O2 and CO2) and lets the cell fold to squeeze through capillaries narrower than its own diameter, both of which optimize gas transport. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 6"},{"q":"Each gram of hemoglobin binds about 1.34 mL of O2. Why does anemia lower oxygen delivery even when arterial PO2 is normal?","choices":["Less hemoglobin is available to bind oxygen, so the oxygen content per volume of blood falls","Anemia saturates the remaining hemoglobin so tightly that it cannot release oxygen","Low hemoglobin somehow raises arterial PO2 but then blocks oxygen from leaving the plasma","Fewer red cells increase plasma viscosity, which physically traps the oxygen under these conditions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oxygen content depends on how much hemoglobin is present, not just on PO2. With fewer grams of hemoglobin, each unit of blood carries fewer milliliters of O2 even though PO2 (and therefore saturation) is normal, so delivery drops. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 21"},{"q":"A markedly elevated hematocrit (as in polycythemia) tends to...","choices":["raise blood viscosity, increasing the resistance the heart must pump against","have no effect on flow because viscosity depends only on the plasma","reduce oxygen-carrying capacity by crowding out the plasma proteins","lower blood viscosity markedly, easing the overall workload placed on the heart"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Viscosity rises steeply as hematocrit climbs; thicker blood increases vascular resistance, so the heart must generate more pressure to maintain flow. This is why very high hematocrits strain the circulation. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 6"},{"q":"Most of the erythropoietin that drives red cell production is released from the...","choices":["spleen, as it continually breaks down senescent red blood cells","liver, in direct response to a rising red blood cell count","bone marrow, whenever iron stores in the body run low","kidneys, in response to reduced oxygen delivery to renal tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The kidneys are the principal source of erythropoietin; specialized cells sense reduced oxygen delivery and secrete EPO, which stimulates marrow erythropoiesis to restore oxygen-carrying capacity. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 14-16"},{"q":"When senescent red cells are broken down, most of their iron is...","choices":["converted into bilirubin so it can be safely stored within the liver","excreted unchanged in the bile as the body's main iron-removal route","recycled by macrophages and returned to the marrow for new hemoglobin","lost through the kidneys and must be fully replaced by diet each day"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Macrophages of the reticuloendothelial system phagocytize old red cells, salvage the iron, and return it (via transferrin) to the marrow, so the body reuses iron efficiently rather than relying on daily intake. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 14-17"},{"q":"Immediately after a vessel is cut, the first hemostatic response is...","choices":["conversion of prothrombin to thrombin throughout the plasma","fibrin cross-linking that seals the wound within seconds","release of erythropoietin to replace the lost red cells","vascular spasm that constricts the vessel and limits blood loss"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The earliest response is immediate vascular spasm (myogenic, neural, and local factors), which narrows the vessel and reduces blood loss even before the platelet plug and coagulation cascade take over. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 34-40"},{"q":"When platelets contact exposed collagen, they become activated and...","choices":["release ADP and thromboxane A2 that recruit and activate additional platelets","release plasmin that immediately begins dissolving the clot as it forms","secrete erythropoietin that stimulates the marrow to replace them","absorb calcium so it cannot participate in the clotting cascade during this particular process"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Activated platelets undergo the release reaction, secreting ADP and thromboxane A2; these recruit and activate more platelets, creating positive feedback that builds the platelet plug at the injury site. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 34"},{"q":"Thrombin is central to coagulation because it...","choices":["acts only on platelets and has no effect on plasma clotting proteins","directly digests fibrin strands to keep the growing clot from spreading","binds circulating vitamin K directly so that new clotting factors can then be manufactured","converts fibrinogen to fibrin and also activates factors that accelerate its own formation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thrombin cleaves fibrinogen into fibrin and additionally activates factors (and platelets) upstream, creating positive feedback that accelerates further thrombin generation and rapidly amplifies the clot. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 40-47"},{"q":"Days after a clot forms, it is dissolved when plasminogen is converted to...","choices":["plasmin, which digests the fibrin meshwork of the clot","thrombin, which reinforces the fibrin meshwork further","fibrinogen, which is redeposited to enlarge the clot","prothrombin activator, which restarts the clotting cascade"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Plasminogen incorporated into the clot is activated to plasmin, a protease that breaks down fibrin (fibrinolysis), clearing the clot once the vessel has healed. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 40-48"},{"q":"Neutrophils are the body's first cellular responders to bacterial infection because they...","choices":["are numerous, circulate ready to act, and rapidly phagocytize bacteria","secrete antibodies that specifically neutralize the invading bacteria","mature slowly in the tissue before they can begin any phagocytosis","live for months in tissues, waiting to present antigen to lymphocytes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophils are the most abundant circulating white cell and are already mature, so they marginate, undergo diapedesis, and phagocytize bacteria within about an hour of infection, making them the earliest cellular defense. — Blood & Immunology, slides 3-13, 28-30"},{"q":"Eosinophils are especially important in defending against...","choices":["fungal spores, which they engulf through receptor-mediated endocytosis","intracellular viruses, which they lyse using secreted perforins","parasitic worms, which they attack by releasing toxic granule proteins","extracellular bacteria, which they clear mainly by opsonization"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Eosinophils specialize against large parasites such as helminths; they attach to the worm and release toxic granule contents (including major basic protein) that damage it, since the parasite is too big to phagocytize. — Blood & Immunology, slide 33"},{"q":"Lymph nodes contribute to immunity mainly by...","choices":["storing mature neutrophils until an infection calls them out","filtering lymph and exposing trapped antigens to resident lymphocytes","secreting digestive enzymes that break down circulating pathogens","manufacturing red blood cells that carry antigen to the spleen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. As lymph percolates through the node, macrophages lining the sinuses trap antigens and present them, while resident B- and T-lymphocytes are positioned to encounter and respond to those antigens, mounting an adaptive response. — Blood & Immunology, slides 22, 39"},{"q":"One way the lymphatic system supports immune surveillance is by...","choices":["absorbing dietary glucose directly and delivering it straight to circulating lymphocytes","producing antibodies within the lymphatic vessels themselves before lymph returns to blood","pumping freshly oxygenated blood directly to the sites of active infection in the tissues","returning interstitial fluid and any captured antigens through nodes before reaching the blood"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lymphatics collect interstitial fluid (and antigens or microbes within it) and route it through lymph nodes for immune screening before returning it to the bloodstream, so pathogens are intercepted en route. — Blood & Immunology, slides 22, 39"},{"q":"Dendritic cells are considered the most potent antigen-presenting cells because they...","choices":["secrete the antibodies that tag pathogens throughout the body for destruction","specialize in capturing antigen and displaying it to activate naive T-cells","store iron recovered from red cells for later hemoglobin synthesis","form the pores that lyse virus-infected host cells directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dendritic cells are distributed throughout tissues, and their principal job is to capture antigen and present it on MHC to naive T-cells, making them the most effective initiators of adaptive responses. — Blood & Immunology, slide 55"},{"q":"Why does loss of helper T-cells (as in advanced HIV) cripple the whole immune response?","choices":["Helper T-cells release cytokines that coordinate both antibody and cytotoxic responses","Helper T-cells are the only cells able to directly phagocytize bacteria","Helper T-cells physically block viruses from entering any host cells in the body","Helper T-cells manufacture essentially all of the body's circulating complement proteins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Helper (CD4) T-cells secrete cytokines/lymphokines that stimulate B-cell antibody production, cytotoxic T-cell activity, and macrophage function; without them, the coordinated adaptive response collapses, which is why CD4 loss is so devastating. — Blood & Immunology, slides 57-58"},{"q":"Which statement about antibody isotypes is correct?","choices":["IgG appears first, while IgM is the antibody of allergic reactions","IgM appears first in a response, while IgG is the most abundant in plasma","IgE is the most abundant plasma antibody and mainly drives agglutination","IgA is produced only after IgG and functions inside infected cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. IgM is the earliest antibody produced in a primary response (and is a large pentamer with many binding sites), whereas IgG makes up roughly three-quarters of plasma immunoglobulin and dominates the later and secondary responses. — Blood & Immunology, slide 51"},{"q":"Receiving preformed antibodies (for example, across the placenta) provides...","choices":["passive immunity that is immediate but temporary","permanent immunity through the recipient's own memory cells","innate immunity that never involves any antibodies","active immunity that is delayed but long-lasting"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Preformed antibodies protect right away because no immune response has to develop, but they are gradually degraded and no memory cells are formed, so the protection is short-lived (weeks). — Blood & Immunology, slide 63"},{"q":"The enormous variety of antibody specificities is generated by...","choices":["the antigen itself instructing the lymphocyte how to fold its antibody","random recombination of gene segments, producing many distinct lymphocyte clones","a single gene that mutates anew during each and every separate infection encountered","the thymus copying antibodies from previously infected individuals"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A limited set of gene segments (cassettes) recombine in many permutations during lymphocyte development, so millions of clones each bearing a unique specificity arise from a modest number of genes. — Blood & Immunology, slide 44"},{"q":"Self-tolerance is maintained largely because lymphocyte clones that strongly react to self are...","choices":["stored in lymph nodes and activated only during infection","coated with antibody so that they cannot reach any host tissue","deleted by apoptosis during development in the thymus or marrow","converted into memory cells that ignore the self-antigen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Developing T-cells in the thymus and B-cells in the marrow that bind self-antigens with high affinity are induced to undergo apoptosis (clonal deletion), removing most self-reactive clones and preventing autoimmunity. — Blood & Immunology, slides 41, 61"},{"q":"Slow waves in gastrointestinal smooth muscle determine the...","choices":["rate at which the exocrine pancreas releases its digestive enzymes into the gut","absolute strength of every contraction regardless of neural input","maximum frequency and timing of contractions, though not every wave triggers one","the rate of gastric acid secretion coming from the stomach's parietal cells during a meal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Slow waves are rhythmic oscillations in membrane potential set by the interstitial cells of Cajal; they set when contractions can occur, but an actual contraction happens only if the wave reaches threshold with spike potentials, so they pace but do not force every contraction. — GI Physiology, slides 35-39"},{"q":"As a meal enters the stomach, receptive relaxation allows it to...","choices":["secrete bile to begin the early emulsification of dietary fat","immediately propel all contents into the duodenum at once","accommodate a large volume with little rise in internal pressure","absorb most nutrients before they reach the small intestine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A vagovagal reflex relaxes the orad stomach (mediated by NO and VIP) so it can expand to hold a meal without a large pressure increase, storing food for gradual processing and controlled emptying. — GI Physiology, slides 44, 46"},{"q":"Cholecystokinin, released in response to fat and protein in the duodenum, acts to...","choices":["contract the gallbladder and stimulate pancreatic enzyme secretion","trigger mass movements that empty the distal colon in the body under most conditions","relax the gallbladder while inhibiting all pancreatic secretion","stimulate parietal cells to secrete their maximal load of acid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. CCK from duodenal I-cells is the principal signal that contracts the gallbladder (and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi) while strongly stimulating pancreatic enzyme release, delivering bile and enzymes to digest fat and protein. — GI Physiology, slides 13-15"},{"q":"Final digestion of disaccharides such as lactose occurs...","choices":["within the pancreatic duct before the sugars reach the intestine","in the mouth, where salivary amylase completes their breakdown","at the brush border, where membrane-bound enzymes split them into monosaccharides","in the stomach, where gastric acid hydrolyzes them completely into free glucose units"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Disaccharidases such as lactase, sucrase, and maltase are fixed on the intestinal brush border; they perform the final step of carbohydrate digestion, splitting disaccharides into absorbable monosaccharides right at the absorptive surface. — GI Physiology, slides 123-125"},{"q":"Enterohepatic circulation conserves bile salts by...","choices":["converting them into cholesterol for storage in the gallbladder","excreting nearly all of them in the feces after a single use","secreting them into the urine for later reabsorption by the kidney","reabsorbing most of them in the ileum and returning them to the liver"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. About 95% of secreted bile salts are actively reabsorbed in the terminal ileum, enter the portal blood, and are taken back up by hepatocytes for re-secretion, so the same pool is reused several times per meal and only a small fraction is lost in feces. — GI Physiology (bile), Ch 71 Liver, slide 25"},{"q":"The pancreatic bicarbonate response to a meal is driven mainly by...","choices":["gastrin, released as protein stretches the stomach wall","insulin, released as blood glucose rises after eating","motilin, released cyclically during the fasting state","secretin, released when acidic chyme lowers the duodenal pH"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When acidic chyme enters the duodenum and pH falls below about 4.5, S-cells release secretin, which stimulates the pancreatic ducts to secrete a bicarbonate-rich fluid that neutralizes the acid and protects the mucosa. — GI Physiology, slides 106-109"},{"q":"The liver prepares bilirubin for excretion by...","choices":["oxidizing it into iron that is then returned to the bone marrow","binding it tightly to intrinsic factor for its absorption in the ileum","conjugating it with glucuronic acid to make it water-soluble for bile","converting it into a bile salt that emulsifies dietary fat"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hepatocytes conjugate lipid-soluble (unconjugated) bilirubin with glucuronic acid, converting it to a water-soluble (conjugated) form that can be excreted into bile; impaired conjugation or excretion causes jaundice. — Ch 71 Liver, slides 13-16"},{"q":"Which statement about protein absorption is correct?","choices":["Protein absorption occurs mainly in the stomach alongside pepsin action","Peptides are absorbed intact into blood without any further breakdown","Only free amino acids can be absorbed, since peptides are never taken up at all","Di- and tripeptides are absorbed by their own carrier, then split inside the cell"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In addition to amino-acid transporters, the enterocyte has a distinct carrier for di- and tripeptides (which is actually faster); once inside, cytoplasmic peptidases hydrolyze these small peptides to free amino acids before they enter the blood. — GI Physiology, slides 127-129"},{"q":"Micelles improve fat absorption by...","choices":["carrying absorbed fat directly into the portal blood as chylomicrons","ferrying poorly soluble fatty acids and monoglycerides to the enterocyte surface","actively transporting whole fat droplets across the cell membrane","chemically converting the triglycerides into small water-soluble sugars for uptake"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bile-salt micelles solubilize the products of fat digestion (fatty acids, monoglycerides, cholesterol) and shuttle them across the unstirred water layer to the brush border, where they are released to diffuse into the enterocyte. — GI Physiology, slides 132-133"},{"q":"By the time colonic contents approach the rectum, the large intestine has primarily...","choices":["added large volumes of bile to emulsify the residual dietary fat still present","secreted digestive enzymes to complete nutrient breakdown","absorbed most of the remaining water and electrolytes to form solid feces","absorbed the bulk of dietary glucose and amino acids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The main function of the colon is to reabsorb water and electrolytes from the residual chyme, progressively concentrating it into formed feces before it reaches the rectum to trigger the defecation reflex. — GI Physiology, slides 59-66"},{"q":"A hallmark synthetic function of the liver is production of...","choices":["plasma proteins such as albumin and most clotting factors","digestive amylase secreted into the small intestine","erythropoietin that stimulates red cell production","insulin and glucagon that closely regulate blood glucose"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The liver synthesizes the majority of plasma proteins, including albumin (the main determinant of colloid osmotic pressure) and most coagulation factors, which is why liver disease produces both edema and bleeding tendencies. — Ch 71 Liver, slides 2, 11"},{"q":"The liver serves as a major storage site for...","choices":["digestive enzymes awaiting release into the duodenum","glycogen, iron, and several fat-soluble vitamins","oxygen bound to hemoglobin for release during exercise","mature lymphocytes ready to fight infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The liver stores glycogen (a glucose reserve), iron (as ferritin), and fat-soluble vitamins such as A and D, acting as a central metabolic warehouse the body can draw on between meals. — Ch 71 Liver, slides 2, 8"},{"q":"Muscle and adipose tissue increase glucose uptake after a meal because insulin...","choices":["converts glucose directly into ATP inside the blood","opens voltage-gated channels that let glucose diffuse freely","stimulates the liver to release stored oxygen","recruits GLUT4 transporters to the cell membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Insulin stimulates translocation of GLUT4 glucose transporters from intracellular vesicles to the plasma membrane of muscle and fat cells, greatly increasing their glucose uptake; this is why these tissues clear glucose after meals in an insulin-dependent way. — Ch 68, slide 4"},{"q":"Phosphorylating glucose to glucose-6-phosphate as it enters a cell serves to...","choices":["signal the pancreas to stop secreting insulin into the bloodstream right away","convert glucose into fat for long-term storage","release energy that the cell immediately uses for contraction","trap glucose inside the cell by preventing it from diffusing back out"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Adding a charged phosphate group keeps glucose from crossing the membrane back out, effectively trapping it inside so it is committed to intracellular metabolism (glycolysis, glycogen synthesis, or the pentose pathway). — Ch 68, slides 6-7"},{"q":"In the Cori cycle, lactate produced by exercising muscle is...","choices":["carried to the liver and converted back to glucose","stored in muscle indefinitely as a backup fuel","converted directly into fatty acids within the muscle","excreted by the kidney as the body's main disposal route"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lactate generated by anaerobic muscle travels in the blood to the liver, where gluconeogenesis converts it back to glucose; that glucose can then return to the muscle, shifting the metabolic burden of lactate disposal to the liver. — Ch 68, slides 25-27; Ch 73, slide 4"},{"q":"Very-low-density lipoprotein (VLDL) is produced by the liver mainly to...","choices":["carry dietary fat absorbed directly from the intestine","return excess cholesterol from tissues back to the liver","export triglycerides, largely made from carbohydrate, to peripheral tissues","deliver dissolved oxygen to the working tissues during periods of heavy exercise"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The liver packages triglycerides (much of it synthesized from excess carbohydrate) into VLDL and exports them to peripheral tissues, where lipoprotein lipase releases fatty acids for use or storage. — Ch 69, slide 6"},{"q":"The body's main route for eliminating cholesterol is...","choices":["exhalation of cholesterol breakdown products as carbon dioxide","storage of all excess cholesterol permanently in muscle","conversion to bile acids that are excreted in the feces","filtration of intact cholesterol by the kidneys into urine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The liver converts cholesterol into bile acids, and cholesterol/bile acids lost in feces represent the body's principal excretory route; this is why binding bile salts in the gut (as fiber does) can lower plasma cholesterol. — Ch 69, slide 36; Ch 71, slides 23, 25"},{"q":"Insulin promotes fat storage in part by...","choices":["inhibiting hormone-sensitive lipase so stored triglyceride is not broken down","blocking lipoprotein lipase so tissues cannot take up fat","activating hormone-sensitive lipase to release fatty acids","stimulating ketone body production from the mobilized fatty acids in the liver"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Insulin suppresses hormone-sensitive lipase in adipocytes, preventing breakdown of stored triglyceride, while it also stimulates lipoprotein lipase and fatty-acid synthesis, together favoring net fat storage in the fed state. — Ch 69, slide 22; Ch 73, slide 15"},{"q":"Nonessential amino acids differ from essential ones in that the body can...","choices":["synthesize them, often by transamination onto a carbon skeleton","store them long-term, unlike the essential amino acids","excrete them directly in the urine, bypassing the whole urea cycle","absorb them without any carrier, unlike essential ones"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nonessential amino acids can be made in the body, typically by transferring an amino group (transamination, using a vitamin B6-derived cofactor) onto an appropriate carbon skeleton such as pyruvate or a TCA intermediate; essential amino acids cannot be synthesized and must come from the diet. — Ch 70, slides 7-11"},{"q":"Amino acids that are degraded to acetyl-CoA or acetoacetyl-CoA are termed ketogenic because they...","choices":["release ammonia that is directly excreted by the lungs","give rise to ketone bodies rather than to new glucose","are used only to build new proteins, never for fuel","can be converted into glucose through gluconeogenesis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ketogenic amino acids feed into acetyl-CoA or acetoacetyl-CoA, which can form ketone bodies but cannot be used to make net glucose (since acetyl-CoA cannot be converted back to pyruvate for gluconeogenesis). — Ch 70, slide 18"},{"q":"Sustained positive energy balance (intake exceeding output) leads to...","choices":["excretion of the extra calories unchanged in the urine","an immediate rise in basal metabolic rate that burns it off","storage of the surplus energy, primarily as body fat","conversion of the excess directly into muscle protein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When intake chronically exceeds expenditure, the extra energy is deposited, overwhelmingly as triglyceride in adipose tissue, which is the body's largest and most efficient energy store; this is the fundamental basis of weight gain. — Ch 72, slides 2-3"},{"q":"Short-term meal termination is promoted by signals such as...","choices":["ghrelin rising steadily throughout the meal","leptin secreted by fat cells over days to weeks","a steady fall in blood glucose occurring throughout the course of the meal","gastric distension and CCK release acting through vagal afferents"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. As the stomach fills, stretch is signaled by vagal afferents, and fat/protein in the duodenum releases CCK; together these short-term signals promote satiety and help end the meal, acting over minutes rather than days. — Ch 72, slide 11"},{"q":"Leptin is best described as a signal that...","choices":["triggers hunger within minutes of the stomach emptying","is released mainly by the stomach in order to help initiate each separate meal","reports the size of fat stores and reduces appetite over the long term","directly digests dietary fat within the lumen of the small intestine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Leptin is secreted by adipocytes in proportion to fat mass; higher stores mean more leptin, which acts on the hypothalamus to reduce appetite and increase expenditure, providing long-term regulation of body weight. — Ch 72, slides 13-15"},{"q":"Which statement about body temperature is correct?","choices":["Skin temperature stays constant while core temperature swings widely","Neither core nor skin temperature is regulated in any way by the hypothalamus at all","Both the core and the skin temperature change together and equally with the surroundings","Core temperature stays nearly constant while skin temperature varies with the environment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The deep-body (core) temperature is tightly regulated and varies by less than about a degree under normal conditions, whereas skin (shell) temperature fluctuates widely as it exchanges heat with the surroundings. — Ch 74, slides 2-4"},{"q":"When ambient temperature exceeds skin temperature, the only effective route of heat loss becomes...","choices":["conduction of heat into the warmer surrounding air","radiation of infrared waves to the surroundings","evaporation of sweat from the skin surface","convection carrying heat away from the body"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Once the environment is warmer than the skin, radiation, conduction, and convection can no longer remove heat (they add it), so evaporation of sweat becomes the body's only effective avenue of heat loss. — Ch 74, slides 6-9"},{"q":"Shivering raises body temperature by...","choices":["producing heat through rapid, involuntary muscle contractions","increasing evaporative water loss from the airways and skin","lowering the hypothalamic set point toward the cold","shunting warm blood from the core to the skin surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Shivering is driven by the hypothalamic motor center for shivering; rapid involuntary muscle contractions generate substantial metabolic heat (potentially several-fold above resting), warming the body in the cold. — Ch 74, slide 15"},{"q":"To conserve heat in the cold, sympathetic signals cause skin blood vessels to...","choices":["constrict, reducing heat transfer from the core to the surface","leak plasma into tissues to insulate the body","dilate widely, bringing much more warm blood to the skin surface","increase sweating to trap a warm surface layer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sympathetic activation produces cutaneous vasoconstriction, cutting blood flow to the skin so that less core heat is carried to the surface where it could be lost; the skin radiator is effectively turned down. — Ch 74, slides 11-14"},{"q":"Indirect calorimetry estimates metabolic rate by measuring...","choices":["the carbon dioxide dissolved in the arterial blood under these conditions","the heat radiated directly from the whole body surface over time","the total nitrogen excreted in the urine over a day","oxygen consumption, since most energy release depends on oxygen use"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Because about 95% of the body's energy release comes from reactions consuming oxygen, oxygen consumption is a reliable proxy for metabolic rate; indirect calorimetry measures O2 use rather than heat directly. — Ch 73, slide 7"},{"q":"Thyroid hormone influences metabolic rate by...","choices":["acting only on skeletal muscle during exercise","having no measurable effect on basal metabolism","raising the resting metabolic rate of most body tissues","lowering metabolic rate to conserve energy during fasting"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Thyroid hormone increases the metabolic activity of nearly all tissues, raising basal metabolic rate and heat production; this is why thyroid status is a major determinant of BMR. — Ch 73, slide 9"},{"q":"One reason men tend to have a higher basal metabolic rate than women is...","choices":["a somewhat lower body temperature that tends to speed up reactions","the absence of any thermic effect from their food","a higher ratio of metabolically active lean muscle to fat","a smaller total body surface area losing less heat"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lean muscle is more metabolically active than fat, and men on average carry a higher muscle-to-fat ratio (linked to testosterone), which raises their resting energy expenditure relative to women. — Ch 73, slides 9, 11"},{"q":"The principal muscle responsible for quiet inspiration is the...","choices":["diaphragm, whose contraction increases the vertical dimension of the thorax","sternocleidomastoid muscle, which is said to be the primary driver at rest","internal intercostal group, which actively pulls the ribs downward","abdominal wall, which pushes the diaphragm upward to draw in air"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. During quiet breathing the diaphragm does most of the work; its contraction flattens the dome and lengthens the thoracic cavity, lowering intrapleural and alveolar pressure so air flows in. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 12-14"},{"q":"During quiet breathing, expiration is normally...","choices":["passive, driven by elastic recoil of the lungs and chest wall","active, requiring forceful contraction of the diaphragm","dependent on surfactant pushing air out of the alveoli","produced entirely by the external intercostal muscles alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. At rest, expiration requires no muscular effort: when the inspiratory muscles relax, the stored elastic recoil of the lungs and chest wall passively returns them to resting volume, pushing air out. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 12-14"},{"q":"The lung's tendency to recoil inward comes from...","choices":["the rigidity of the surrounding bony rib cage and vertebral column","active contraction of smooth muscle lining each alveolus","elastic fibers in lung tissue together with alveolar surface tension","positive pressure generated within the pleural space during this particular process"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Two factors create recoil: the elastin and collagen of lung tissue, and the surface tension of the fluid lining the alveoli. Surface tension actually contributes the larger share of recoil, which is why surfactant (which reduces it) is so important. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 19-20"},{"q":"A lung that is stiffer than normal (low compliance) requires...","choices":["less effort to inflate because it fills more readily","a greater change in pressure to achieve a given increase in volume","no change at all in the work of breathing under these conditions","a smaller pressure change to reach the same volume in the body under most conditions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Compliance is volume change per unit pressure change; a low-compliance (stiff) lung needs a larger pressure change to inflate by the same amount, which increases the work of breathing. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 14, 19"},{"q":"Tidal volume refers to the amount of air...","choices":["moved into or out of the lungs during a single normal breath","remaining in the lungs after the most forceful expiration","in the lungs at the very end of a maximal inspiration","that can be inspired beyond a normal quiet inspiration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tidal volume is the volume inspired or expired with each ordinary quiet breath (about 500 mL at rest); the other volumes describe reserves beyond it or the air that cannot be exhaled. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 27-31"},{"q":"Functional residual capacity is the volume of air...","choices":["inspired maximally after a normal inspiration","expelled during a single normal resting breath","moved into and out per minute during ordinary breathing","remaining in the lungs after a normal, quiet expiration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. FRC is the air left in the lungs at the end of a normal tidal expiration, equal to expiratory reserve volume plus residual volume; it represents the resting equilibrium volume where lung recoil and chest-wall recoil balance. — Pulmonary I & II, slide 30"},{"q":"Alveolar ventilation is always less than total minute ventilation because...","choices":["the alveoli are able to exchange gas only during the expiratory phase","part of each breath fills conducting airways that do no gas exchange","oxygen is consumed before air can reach the alveoli","dead-space air is the only air that reaches the blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Each tidal breath must first fill the anatomic dead space (~150 mL of conducting airway), and that air never reaches the alveoli for exchange, so the ventilation available for gas exchange (alveolar ventilation) is less than the total minute ventilation. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 32-35"},{"q":"Pulmonary surfactant is produced by...","choices":["alveolar macrophages that also clear inhaled debris","type I alveolar cells, forming the main gas-exchange surface","goblet cells lining the large conducting airways under these conditions","type II alveolar cells and lowers surface tension in the alveoli"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Type II alveolar (pneumocyte) cells secrete surfactant, a phospholipid-rich material that lowers alveolar surface tension, stabilizes alveoli of different sizes, and reduces the work of breathing. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 22-23"},{"q":"Ventilation-perfusion matching improves gas exchange by...","choices":["aligning airflow and blood flow so alveoli are neither wasted nor underused","sending essentially all blood flow to the very apex of the upright lung","ensuring blood always flows to alveoli that receive no air during this particular process","keeping ventilation constant regardless of local blood flow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Efficient exchange requires that alveolar ventilation and capillary perfusion be matched; local mechanisms (such as hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction) direct blood toward well-ventilated alveoli, minimizing both wasted ventilation and wasted perfusion. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 43, 64-65"},{"q":"Which correctly lists a barrier that O2 crosses at the respiratory membrane?","choices":["several thick layers of contractile smooth muscle that surround each individual alveolus","a thick plate of cartilage that structurally supports each alveolar wall","a mucus-secreting ciliated epithelium lining the alveolus","the alveolar epithelium, a thin interstitial space, and the capillary endothelium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oxygen diffuses through the surfactant/fluid layer, the alveolar epithelium, a fused basement-membrane/interstitial space, and the capillary endothelium (then plasma and red-cell membrane). The whole barrier is only about 0.2 micrometers thick. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 57-60"},{"q":"The basic rhythm of quiet breathing is generated in the...","choices":["carotid bodies located at the carotid bifurcation","phrenic nerve endings within the diaphragm","medullary respiratory centers of the brainstem","cerebral cortex during all resting breaths"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The fundamental respiratory rhythm arises from networks in the medulla oblongata; these brainstem centers drive the cycle of inspiration and expiration, with modulation from the pons, chemoreceptors, and higher centers. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 88-90"},{"q":"Under normal conditions, the strongest minute-to-minute stimulus for breathing is...","choices":["stretch of the diaphragm occurring during each and every inspiration","a rise in arterial CO2, sensed largely by central chemoreceptors","a rise in arterial blood pressure at the aortic arch","a small fall in arterial oxygen sensed by the medulla"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Small increases in arterial CO2 (via the H+ they generate in brain fluid) powerfully stimulate central chemoreceptors, making CO2 the dominant normal drive to breathe; the oxygen drive becomes important only when PO2 falls substantially. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 91-92, 96"},{"q":"The Bohr effect describes how increased CO2 and H+ in active tissue...","choices":["shift the curve rightward, helping hemoglobin release more oxygen","shift the curve leftward, making hemoglobin hold oxygen tightly","convert oxygen into bicarbonate for transport in the body under most conditions","prevent hemoglobin from binding any oxygen in the lungs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The Bohr effect is the rightward shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin curve caused by higher CO2, higher H+ (lower pH), and higher temperature in metabolically active tissue; the reduced affinity promotes oxygen unloading exactly where it is needed. — Pulmonary I & II, slide 81"},{"q":"Factors such as increased temperature, CO2, and acidity in metabolically active tissue tend to...","choices":["increase hemoglobin's affinity so it retains its oxygen","block hemoglobin from picking up any oxygen at all in the lungs","reduce oxygen unloading, sparing oxygen for later use","promote oxygen unloading from hemoglobin where it is most needed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. These conditions mark actively metabolizing tissue and shift the oxygen-hemoglobin curve rightward, lowering affinity so hemoglobin releases more oxygen precisely at the tissues that are working hardest. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 80-81"},{"q":"Two blood samples can have the same arterial PO2 but different oxygen content if they differ in...","choices":["the pH, which does not affect oxygen content","hemoglobin concentration, which sets how much oxygen the blood carries","the temperature of the surrounding plasma alone, independent of hemoglobin","the amount of dissolved carbon dioxide only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. PO2 determines saturation, but total oxygen content also depends on how much hemoglobin is present. An anemic sample and a normal sample at the same PO2 can be equally saturated yet carry very different amounts of oxygen because they differ in hemoglobin. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 76-77"},{"q":"As bicarbonate leaves the red cell in exchange for chloride (the chloride shift), the purpose is to...","choices":["maintain electrical balance as CO2 is converted for transport","generate ATP for the red cell's own energy needs","bind oxygen more tightly to the hemoglobin inside the red cell","prevent bicarbonate from ever entering the plasma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Carbonic anhydrase generates bicarbonate inside the red cell; as HCO3- diffuses out into plasma, chloride moves in to preserve electrical neutrality (the chloride shift), allowing large amounts of CO2 to be carried as plasma bicarbonate. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 86-87"},{"q":"Carbon dioxide is transported in blood in three forms; the largest fraction travels as...","choices":["carbonic acid contained within the red blood cells","bicarbonate ions dissolved in the plasma","dissolved CO2 gas in the plasma","carbaminohemoglobin bound to globin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The majority of CO2 (about 70%) is carried as bicarbonate formed after carbonic anhydrase hydrates CO2 in red cells; smaller fractions travel bound to hemoglobin (carbamino, ~23%) or dissolved as free CO2 (~7%). — Pulmonary I & II, slides 83-86"},{"q":"When metabolic acid accumulates in the blood, the respiratory system compensates by...","choices":["increasing bicarbonate excretion through the lungs","holding ventilation constant since breathing cannot affect pH","increasing ventilation to blow off CO2 and raise pH","decreasing ventilation to retain CO2 and lower pH"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Excess H+ stimulates ventilation; by exhaling more CO2, the body reduces carbonic acid, which raises pH back toward normal. This respiratory compensation partially offsets a metabolic acidosis. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 91-92, 100"},{"q":"Central chemoreceptors respond to blood CO2 indirectly because CO2...","choices":["is sensed only after it has been converted into oxygen in the brain","cannot cross into the brain and must be sensed peripherally","binds directly to the chemoreceptor without any change","crosses into brain fluid and forms H+, which the receptors detect"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. CO2 readily crosses the blood-brain barrier into the brain extracellular fluid, where carbonic anhydrase converts it to carbonic acid and then H+; the central chemoreceptors actually sense that H+, giving CO2 its powerful (if indirect) effect on ventilation. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 91, 96-98"},{"q":"Oxygen moves from capillary blood into a metabolizing cell because...","choices":["the cell actively pumps oxygen across its membrane","carbon dioxide actively pushes the oxygen into the cell","blood PO2 is always lower than the cell's PO2","the cell's PO2 is lower than that of the arriving blood"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Actively metabolizing cells consume oxygen, keeping intracellular PO2 low; oxygen therefore diffuses down its partial-pressure gradient from the higher-PO2 capillary blood into the cell, with no active transport required. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 71-73"}]};
